  <article class="research-article">
    <h1 class="research-claim">
      The SaaSpocalypse was a multiple,<br />not a market.
    </h1>
    <p class="research-dek">{dek}</p>
    <hr class="research-rule" />
    <h2 class="research-section">The multiple</h2>
    <p>In February 2026 a Jefferies trader named a software selloff the SaaSpocalypse. The label stuck because it sounded like a funeral. It was a re-rate.</p>
    <p>Say which index you mean, or the sentence is not true. Equal-weight medians describe the typical name. Averages get pulled around by a handful of platforms. The top ten are a different asset class.</p>
    <p>On 14 August 2026, Meritech’s Software Pulse put the overall median implied ARR multiple at 4.3x, fifty percent below its pre-ZIRP median of 8.7x. The top ten sat at 19.9x, twenty percent <em>above</em> their pre-ZIRP 16.6x. Fourteen public software names traded above 10x next-twelve-months revenue. Sixty-three percent traded under 5x. The Meritech Software Index was roughly flat year to date and up 37 percent over the prior six months.</p>
    <p>Bessemer’s Nasdaq Emerging Cloud Index, fetched 25 August 2026, printed an <em>average</em> (not median) revenue multiple of 7.8x on 20.9 percent average growth and about $2.4 trillion of market cap. That is the mega-cap tape, not the median name.</p>
    <p>SaaS Capital’s equal-weight index peaked near 16.9x in August 2021 and printed a local low near 6.25x in November 2022. In June 2025 the firm described a 6–8x band as a “new normal.” That band is commentary, not an official index KPI. After the early-2026 AI re-rate, SaaS Capital’s own language was “decade-plus lows.” Treat any single-day 3-handle print as a file citation, not a law.</p>
    <p>Prices and multiples are not the same story. Through 13 August 2026, IGV’s total return was +0.58 percent year to date and 9.75 percent below its 22 September 2025 high. The cash index had mostly walked back the scare. The equal-weight multiple had not.</p>
    <p>That is a barbell, not a crash. Growth still buys more multiple than free-cash-flow margin. Meritech’s Rule of 40 split in mid-August was roughly 12–15x on the faster-growth cohort versus 4.5–4.8x on the high-FCF, slower-growth names.</p>
    <p>The 2022 episode was the same shape with a different driver. Rates went up, growth slowed, multiples compressed on the order of 60–75 percent depending on the screen. Revenue did not vanish. People mostly called that one the SaaSacre. Do not paste 2022 onto 2026 and call it a sequel.</p>
